1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,125
Senior Angular interview preparation, lesson 4.

2
00:00:03,125 --> 00:00:07,292
Angular Component APIs: Native Semantics and Consumer Contracts.

3
00:00:09,500 --> 00:00:10,864
Design from consumer tasks

4
00:00:11,989 --> 00:00:14,106
Welcome to the component API lesson.

5
00:00:14,106 --> 00:00:17,282
Our four questions concern a button that saves twice,

6
00:00:17,282 --> 00:00:19,753
an input wrapper that hides native capabilities,

7
00:00:19,753 --> 00:00:23,282
a card with too much configuration, and a checkbox that

8
00:00:23,282 --> 00:00:24,693
violates the forms contract.

9
00:00:24,693 --> 00:00:28,575
They share a root concern: a shared component should make the

10
00:00:28,575 --> 00:00:32,105
supported task clear and preserve the behavior consumers reasonably expect.

11
00:00:33,479 --> 00:00:35,378
Design from consumer tasks · 2

12
00:00:36,503 --> 00:00:38,455
Begin with a representative consumer template.

13
00:00:38,455 --> 00:00:41,059
What does the team bind, which events do

14
00:00:41,059 --> 00:00:43,337
they handle, and where does state live?

15
00:00:43,337 --> 00:00:46,917
Then examine the actual host element and any internal native element.

16
00:00:46,917 --> 00:00:49,846
A label, a disabled attribute, or an identifier on

17
00:00:49,846 --> 00:00:52,775
a wrapper does not automatically reach the internal control.

18
00:00:52,775 --> 00:00:56,030
An API that appears convenient can force dozens of forwarding

19
00:00:56,030 --> 00:00:59,285
inputs as soon as another team needs a native capability.

20
00:01:00,660 --> 00:01:03,126
Design from consumer tasks · 3

21
00:01:04,251 --> 00:01:08,770
A senior answer considers the interface as well as individual bugs.

22
00:01:08,770 --> 00:01:12,879
Explain the smallest useful contract, show how to use it,

23
00:01:12,879 --> 00:01:16,988
identify unsupported combinations, and describe the migration from today's behavior.

24
00:01:16,988 --> 00:01:20,276
Compatibility includes native events, form state, projected content,

25
00:01:20,276 --> 00:01:23,974
styling hooks, and test APIs, not just exported TypeScript

26
00:01:23,974 --> 00:01:24,384
names.

27
00:01:25,760 --> 00:01:27,277
Inputs express allowed states

28
00:01:28,403 --> 00:01:31,057
Use input types that express supported values.

29
00:01:31,057 --> 00:01:35,609
A string input for button kind accepts values the stylesheet cannot represent.

30
00:01:35,609 --> 00:01:38,643
A union of primary, secondary, and danger tells

31
00:01:38,643 --> 00:01:41,298
the compiler and editor what is supported.

32
00:01:41,298 --> 00:01:45,091
Defaults should be intentional, and required inputs should be reserved

33
00:01:45,091 --> 00:01:48,505
for values without which the component cannot function meaningfully.

34
00:01:49,880 --> 00:01:52,286
Inputs express allowed states · 2

35
00:01:53,411 --> 00:01:55,564
Boolean attributes need careful handling.

36
00:01:55,564 --> 00:01:59,009
In HTML, disabled with no value expresses presence.

37
00:01:59,009 --> 00:02:03,746
A plain string read as an empty value is falsy in

38
00:02:03,746 --> 00:02:08,052
JavaScript, which can make an apparently disabled wrapper remain clickable.

39
00:02:08,052 --> 00:02:11,066
Angular's booleanAttribute transform gives predictable attribute-style coercion.

40
00:02:11,066 --> 00:02:16,665
Explain which values it accepts and test both static attributes and bound booleans.

41
00:02:18,040 --> 00:02:20,233
Inputs express allowed states · 3

42
00:02:21,358 --> 00:02:23,917
Inputs are data coming from the consumer.

43
00:02:23,917 --> 00:02:28,670
Avoid silently copying them into an unrelated local field that only initializes once.

44
00:02:28,670 --> 00:02:31,595
If the value is derived, use a derivation.

45
00:02:31,595 --> 00:02:36,347
If the child commits local state, a model can express a two-way contract.

46
00:02:36,347 --> 00:02:39,272
If the parent must approve a proposed change,

47
00:02:39,272 --> 00:02:42,928
keep a controlled input and emit a request rather than

48
00:02:42,928 --> 00:02:43,660
committing first.

49
00:02:43,660 --> 00:02:46,584
The signals lesson develops that distinction in depth.

50
00:02:47,960 --> 00:02:49,705
Native events and component outputs

51
00:02:50,830 --> 00:02:55,017
A native click bubbles from an internal button through the component host.

52
00:02:55,017 --> 00:02:58,157
If a component also emits an output called click,

53
00:02:58,157 --> 00:03:00,948
a consumer can receive an unexpected second notification.

54
00:03:00,948 --> 00:03:05,484
Before inventing an event, ask whether the native event already expresses the action.

55
00:03:05,484 --> 00:03:08,625
Ordinary button activation usually needs no custom click output.

56
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:12,439
Native events and component outputs · 2

57
00:03:13,564 --> 00:03:18,441
Reserve component outputs for domain transitions that are not already represented by native events.

58
00:03:18,441 --> 00:03:22,274
A selected value change or a dismissal request can be useful.

59
00:03:22,274 --> 00:03:26,803
Avoid names that collide with native events such as input, change, focus, blur,

60
00:03:26,803 --> 00:03:31,680
or keydown unless the component intentionally documents that contract and its interaction with bubbling.

61
00:03:31,680 --> 00:03:35,165
A lint rule can enforce the library's event naming policy.

62
00:03:36,540 --> 00:03:39,253
Native events and component outputs · 3

63
00:03:40,378 --> 00:03:43,867
Do not distinguish keyboard and pointer activation by guessing

64
00:03:43,867 --> 00:03:46,968
from an event detail without considering assistive technology.

65
00:03:46,968 --> 00:03:51,620
Consumers may legitimately need a native MouseEvent or KeyboardEvent for specialized behavior,

66
00:03:51,620 --> 00:03:56,272
but business logic should ordinarily respond to activation independently of the device.

67
00:03:56,272 --> 00:03:59,762
Keep telemetry requirements separate from whether the action succeeds.

68
00:03:59,762 --> 00:04:02,475
Test one activation produces one consumer action,

69
00:04:02,475 --> 00:04:05,577
and test disabled behavior through the real native

70
00:04:05,577 --> 00:04:05,964
control.

71
00:04:07,340 --> 00:04:10,426
The native host can be the API

72
00:04:11,551 --> 00:04:16,008
An attribute directive on a native input can style and enhance it while

73
00:04:16,008 --> 00:04:20,466
retaining autocomplete, inputmode, maxlength, name, id, validation, browser autofill, labels, and forms integration.

74
00:04:20,466 --> 00:04:23,895
Consumers do not need a wrapper to forward every property.

75
00:04:23,895 --> 00:04:26,981
A component with an attribute selector can also enhance

76
00:04:26,981 --> 00:04:29,725
a native element when a template is needed.

77
00:04:31,100 --> 00:04:34,567
The native host can be the API · 2

78
00:04:35,693 --> 00:04:39,160
A wrapper can still be appropriate for a genuinely composite control,

79
00:04:39,160 --> 00:04:42,943
an overlay-based picker, or a widget whose focusable element needs coordinated internal

80
00:04:42,943 --> 00:04:43,258
structure.

81
00:04:43,258 --> 00:04:44,835
The trade-off should be explicit.

82
00:04:44,835 --> 00:04:47,041
If the wrapper hides the native element,

83
00:04:47,041 --> 00:04:49,563
it needs a clear forwarding and focus contract.

84
00:04:49,563 --> 00:04:53,662
A request for twenty-three new inputs often signals that the abstraction boundary is

85
00:04:53,662 --> 00:04:57,445
in the wrong place rather than that twenty-three more inputs are needed.

86
00:04:58,820 --> 00:05:02,620
The native host can be the API · 3

87
00:05:03,745 --> 00:05:08,029
Shared behavior can be factored through directives and the directive composition API.

88
00:05:08,029 --> 00:05:13,027
Inputs and outputs from host directives are exposed explicitly, and composition is established statically.

89
00:05:13,027 --> 00:05:15,526
This helps reuse focus or disabled logic,

90
00:05:15,526 --> 00:05:19,096
but it cannot automatically forward every arbitrary native attribute into

91
00:05:19,096 --> 00:05:20,167
a hidden descendant.

92
00:05:20,167 --> 00:05:25,165
Choose the host element first, then use composition to share behavior at that boundary.

93
00:05:26,540 --> 00:05:28,148
Composition for rich content

94
00:05:29,273 --> 00:05:33,535
A card with separate inputs for every badge, action, heading,

95
00:05:33,535 --> 00:05:37,797
image, and body style accumulates combinations the library must maintain.

96
00:05:37,797 --> 00:05:43,338
Content projection lets consumers supply real HTML and application components within documented slots.

97
00:05:43,338 --> 00:05:47,175
The library owns layout and style while the application

98
00:05:47,175 --> 00:05:50,585
owns content, heading level, links, and business actions.

99
00:05:51,960 --> 00:05:54,469
Composition for rich content · 2

100
00:05:55,594 --> 00:06:01,524
Projection preserves Angular bindings and native semantics better than passing an HTML string.

101
00:06:01,524 --> 00:06:04,261
Angular sanitization helps protect unsafe bindings,

102
00:06:04,261 --> 00:06:08,367
but sanitization does not turn arbitrary innerHTML into a

103
00:06:08,367 --> 00:06:09,736
well-designed content API.

104
00:06:09,736 --> 00:06:14,754
A body string cannot conveniently carry application directives or typed interactions.

105
00:06:14,754 --> 00:06:20,685
Make image alternatives and headings explicit consumer responsibilities when the content is projected.

106
00:06:22,060 --> 00:06:24,470
Composition for rich content · 3

107
00:06:25,595 --> 00:06:28,406
Configuration remains valuable for uniform repeated data.

108
00:06:28,406 --> 00:06:31,620
A table's column definitions are naturally data driven,

109
00:06:31,620 --> 00:06:35,636
and a menu can often be generated from a typed

110
00:06:35,636 --> 00:06:36,440
item model.

111
00:06:36,440 --> 00:06:40,858
Offer template customization at the points where the structure truly varies.

112
00:06:40,858 --> 00:06:44,071
Avoid replacing every configuration object with dozens of

113
00:06:44,071 --> 00:06:47,284
unstructured slots; explain the consistency versus flexibility trade-off.

114
00:06:48,660 --> 00:06:50,208
Forms have two data directions

115
00:06:51,333 --> 00:06:54,417
A ControlValueAccessor adapts a custom control to Angular Forms.

116
00:06:54,417 --> 00:06:57,501
The forms model calls writeValue to update the view.

117
00:06:57,501 --> 00:07:00,243
The component calls the registered onChange callback after

118
00:07:00,243 --> 00:07:02,985
a user change to update the forms model.

119
00:07:02,985 --> 00:07:04,356
These are opposite directions.

120
00:07:04,356 --> 00:07:07,440
Calling onChange from writeValue creates a feedback path and

121
00:07:07,440 --> 00:07:10,525
can mark a programmatic reset as a user edit.

122
00:07:11,900 --> 00:07:14,461
Forms have two data directions · 2

123
00:07:15,586 --> 00:07:18,718
The registered onTouched callback reports the appropriate touch

124
00:07:18,718 --> 00:07:21,459
interaction, commonly blur for a single input.

125
00:07:21,459 --> 00:07:24,983
The disabled-state hook must update the actual interactive element.

126
00:07:24,983 --> 00:07:30,073
Signals or markForCheck make model-to-view updates observable under OnPush and zoneless change detection.

127
00:07:30,073 --> 00:07:32,814
Normalize null when reset can supply it,

128
00:07:32,814 --> 00:07:36,730
and document whether a checkbox supports an indeterminate state separately

129
00:07:36,730 --> 00:07:37,905
from its value.

130
00:07:39,280 --> 00:07:41,561
Forms have two data directions · 3

131
00:07:42,686 --> 00:07:44,640
Test through a real FormControl host.

132
00:07:44,640 --> 00:07:48,876
A programmatic value write should update the view while the form stays pristine.

133
00:07:48,876 --> 00:07:52,133
A user change should update the value and dirty state.

134
00:07:52,133 --> 00:07:53,762
Blur should make it touched.

135
00:07:53,762 --> 00:07:56,043
Disabling the control should prevent user interaction.

136
00:07:56,043 --> 00:07:59,627
These tests verify the contract; a test that only invokes toggle

137
00:07:59,627 --> 00:08:02,884
on the component misses the connection to the forms engine.

138
00:08:04,260 --> 00:08:07,017
Signal forms are a separate integration contract

139
00:08:08,143 --> 00:08:09,884
Reactive forms use ControlValueAccessor.

140
00:08:09,884 --> 00:08:13,802
Signal-based forms have their own custom-control interfaces and bindings.

141
00:08:13,802 --> 00:08:19,025
Do not assume that a value accessor automatically implements every signal-forms capability.

142
00:08:19,025 --> 00:08:24,685
Check the API for the supported Angular version and choose the integration explicitly.

143
00:08:26,060 --> 00:08:29,583
Signal forms are a separate integration contract · 2

144
00:08:30,708 --> 00:08:33,882
For a value control, the signal-forms interface exposes

145
00:08:33,882 --> 00:08:37,057
a writable model value with supported state inputs.

146
00:08:37,057 --> 00:08:40,232
Checkbox-like controls use the corresponding checked control contract.

147
00:08:40,232 --> 00:08:44,598
The form field binding coordinates the form model with that control.

148
00:08:44,598 --> 00:08:48,963
Disabled, readonly, touched, validation, and accessibility state need to remain coherent.

149
00:08:48,963 --> 00:08:54,122
If the library supports both forms systems, document and test each integration rather

150
00:08:54,122 --> 00:08:58,885
than forcing them to write the same state through two competing paths.

151
00:09:00,260 --> 00:09:03,543
Signal forms are a separate integration contract · 3

152
00:09:04,668 --> 00:09:08,681
Version support matters because these interfaces can differ across Angular releases.

153
00:09:08,681 --> 00:09:11,599
Our complete examples use the installed framework version.

154
00:09:11,599 --> 00:09:14,518
A library claiming support for older majors must

155
00:09:14,518 --> 00:09:17,072
test its public interfaces against those majors.

156
00:09:17,072 --> 00:09:19,991
This is a compatibility claim, not something a

157
00:09:19,991 --> 00:09:22,545
successful build on one version can prove.

158
00:09:23,920 --> 00:09:26,227
Migrate with examples and compatibility tests

159
00:09:27,352 --> 00:09:31,337
Removing an output can change what an existing consumer receives.

160
00:09:31,337 --> 00:09:34,923
Adding a union can reject values that compiled yesterday.

161
00:09:34,923 --> 00:09:39,305
Moving a wrapper to a native attribute selector changes the template.

162
00:09:39,305 --> 00:09:42,493
These improvements need a release and migration strategy.

163
00:09:42,493 --> 00:09:45,282
Inventory current usage, publish the preferred API,

164
00:09:45,282 --> 00:09:48,868
retain compatibility where practical, and give teams a clear

165
00:09:48,868 --> 00:09:49,664
removal window.

166
00:09:51,040 --> 00:09:54,175
Migrate with examples and compatibility tests · 2

167
00:09:55,301 --> 00:09:58,557
Document the consumer-facing form in examples that compile.

168
00:09:58,557 --> 00:10:03,034
Show native events, labels, forms binding, projected slots, and disabled behavior.

169
00:10:03,034 --> 00:10:07,511
A component harness should expose meaningful tasks instead of internal classes

170
00:10:07,511 --> 00:10:11,581
so a markup refactor does not break every product's tests.

171
00:10:11,581 --> 00:10:16,465
Treat that harness as a supported API with its own compatibility obligations.

172
00:10:17,840 --> 00:10:20,693
Migrate with examples and compatibility tests · 3

173
00:10:21,818 --> 00:10:25,028
During the questions, trace a reported bug from the

174
00:10:25,028 --> 00:10:27,881
consumer template to the actual element or callback.

175
00:10:27,881 --> 00:10:30,734
Identify whether the problem is coercion, event duplication,

176
00:10:30,734 --> 00:10:33,588
hidden capabilities, excessive configuration, or reversed data flow.

177
00:10:33,588 --> 00:10:38,224
Then propose a correction and a test that would have failed before it.

178
00:10:38,224 --> 00:10:42,505
This makes the answer reviewable rather than a list of preferred patterns.

