287
00:17:39,364 --> 00:17:42,708
What other output names would you ban in a

288
00:17:42,708 --> 00:17:45,681
component library, and how would you enforce it?

289
00:17:50,849 --> 00:17:54,127
Flag outputs that collide with native DOM events such

290
00:17:54,127 --> 00:17:57,406
as click, input, change, focus, blur, keydown, and submit.

291
00:17:57,406 --> 00:18:01,413
A library lint rule can inspect output declarations and aliases against

292
00:18:01,413 --> 00:18:05,056
a maintained event-name set, with reviewed exceptions for intentional contracts.

293
00:18:05,056 --> 00:18:10,156
Prefer domain names such as selectionChange or dismissed when a new event is needed.

294
00:18:10,156 --> 00:18:12,706
Most native activation needs no wrapper output.

295
00:18:12,706 --> 00:18:15,620
Test bubbling and output behavior from the consumer

296
00:18:15,620 --> 00:18:18,535
host rather than only checking the declaration name.

297
00:18:19,910 --> 00:18:22,389
API-001 · Follow-up 2

298
00:18:23,513 --> 00:18:27,909
A team needs to know how the button was activated (mouse, keyboard).

299
00:18:27,909 --> 00:18:29,741
Does that justify an output?

300
00:18:35,068 --> 00:18:39,670
A consumer can handle the native event when device details are truly needed.

301
00:18:39,670 --> 00:18:43,210
That does not by itself justify emitting another click event.

302
00:18:43,210 --> 00:18:47,458
Keyboard and assistive-technology activation do not always map cleanly to a simple

303
00:18:47,458 --> 00:18:51,706
mouse-versus-keyboard label, so avoid making business behavior depend on a guessed source.

304
00:18:51,706 --> 00:18:54,184
If a domain event needs additional context,

305
00:18:54,184 --> 00:18:57,016
define a distinct typed payload and document its

306
00:18:57,016 --> 00:18:57,370
semantics.

307
00:18:57,370 --> 00:18:59,849
Verify one activation still produces one action.

308
