API-002 · Interview question

344
00:21:20,161 --> 00:21:23,589
What is the underlying design problem behind all 23 requests?

345
00:21:23,589 --> 00:21:26,332
Propose a different design, and explain its trade-offs

346
00:21:26,332 --> 00:21:28,733
and how you would migrate to it.

347
00:21:35,383 --> 00:21:40,033
API-002 · The problem: the component owns the native element

348
00:21:41,157 --> 00:21:44,548
The real <input> is hidden inside the component's template,

349
00:21:44,548 --> 00:21:47,939
so every native capability (over a hundred attributes, properties,

350
00:21:47,939 --> 00:21:51,706
events and methods) is unavailable unless the library re-exposes it

351
00:21:51,706 --> 00:21:52,460
by hand.

352
00:21:52,460 --> 00:21:56,604
Each request is a symptom; adding inputs one by one never

353
00:21:56,604 --> 00:22:00,371
ends, and every forwarded attribute is more API to maintain.

354
00:22:01,747 --> 00:22:06,144
API-002 · The problem: the component owns the native element

355
00:22:07,269 --> 00:22:12,010
Worse, attributes on the host go to the wrong element. id and aria-label

356
00:22:12,010 --> 00:22:16,752
land on <ui-input>, which has no role and cannot take focus. <label for="email">

357
00:22:16,752 --> 00:22:21,494
points at the host, so clicking the label does nothing and the input

358
00:22:21,494 --> 00:22:25,872
has no accessible name. formControlName has no value accessor to bind to.

359
00:22:27,247 --> 00:22:31,634
API-002 · The alternative: put the library on the native element

360
00:22:32,759 --> 00:22:35,265
Use an attribute selector, so the consumer writes

361
00:22:35,265 --> 00:22:37,772
the native element and the library enhances it:

362
00:22:39,147 --> 00:22:43,240
