375
00:23:24,387 --> 00:23:30,071
API-002 · The alternative: put the library on the native element

376
00:23:31,196 --> 00:23:34,582
Every native attribute, event and method now works,

377
00:23:34,582 --> 00:23:38,815
with no library code: autocomplete, inputmode, (blur), focus(), <label for>,

378
00:23:38,815 --> 00:23:39,239
aria-.

379
00:23:39,239 --> 00:23:43,048
Angular Forms' built-in value accessors apply to the native

380
00:23:43,048 --> 00:23:46,435
input, so formControl, formControlName and ngModel work too.

381
00:23:46,435 --> 00:23:51,091
The library adds only what it owns: styling hooks and state.

382
00:23:52,467 --> 00:23:57,360
API-002 · The alternative: put the library on the native element

383
00:23:58,484 --> 00:24:02,043
The same applies to buttons and links: button[uiButton],

384
00:24:02,043 --> 00:24:05,602
a[uiButton] keeps native semantics, type, disabled and href,

385
00:24:05,602 --> 00:24:08,272
and avoids the bugs in API-001.

386
00:24:09,647 --> 00:24:11,727
API-002 · Trade-offs

387
00:24:12,853 --> 00:24:16,839
No surrounding markup. <input> is a void element: the directive cannot

388
00:24:16,839 --> 00:24:20,826
render a label, an icon or an error message around it.

389
00:24:20,826 --> 00:24:24,813
Those belong to a container component, for example <ui-form-field> that projects

390
00:24:24,813 --> 00:24:28,075
a label, the input, a hint and an error,

391
00:24:28,075 --> 00:24:32,061
and connects their ids (it can find the directive with contentChild(InputDirective)).

392
00:24:32,061 --> 00:24:36,048
This is the split Angular Material uses between mat-form-field and matInput.

393
00:24:36,048 --> 00:24:36,410
Styles.

394
00:24:36,410 --> 00:24:39,310
A directive has no stylesheet of its own.

395
00:24:39,310 --> 00:24:44,384
Styles ship as a global stylesheet or with the form-field component, keyed on .ui-input.

396
00:24:44,384 --> 00:24:45,109
Shared namespace.

397
00:24:45,109 --> 00:24:48,733
Directive inputs sit beside native attributes on the same element.

398
00:24:48,733 --> 00:24:53,445
Choose names that will not collide with future HTML attributes, or prefix them.

399
00:24:53,445 --> 00:24:54,169
Less control.

400
00:24:54,169 --> 00:24:57,431
Consumers can now put any attribute on the input,

401
00:24:57,431 --> 00:25:00,331
including ones that conflict with the design system.

402
00:25:00,331 --> 00:25:05,042
That is usually the right trade for a shared library: the platform's API

403
00:25:05,042 --> 00:25:09,392
is better documented and more stable than anything the library could write.

404
00:25:10,767 --> 00:25:12,893
API-002 · Migration

405
00:25:14,018 --> 00:25:19,332
Replacing <ui-input> with <input uiInput> is a breaking change for every call site:

406
00:25:20,707 --> 00:25:24,160
API-002 · Migration · item 1

407
00:25:25,285 --> 00:25:29,268
Minor: ship uiInput and <ui-form-field> alongside the old component,

408
00:25:29,268 --> 00:25:32,808
and mark <ui-input> deprecated in documentation, JSDoc (@deprecated,

409
00:25:32,808 --> 00:25:36,792
which editors show with a strike-through) and the changelog.

410
00:25:38,167 --> 00:25:41,060
API-002 · Migration · item 2

411
00:25:42,185 --> 00:25:45,398
Migration: an ng update schematic that rewrites the common patterns,

412
00:25:45,398 --> 00:25:48,612
and a list of call sites it could not convert.

413
00:25:49,987 --> 00:25:53,291
API-002 · Migration · item 3

414
00:25:54,416 --> 00:25:56,057
Next major: remove <ui-input>.

415
00:25:56,057 --> 00:26:01,392
Freeze its feature requests in the meantime, so effort goes into the replacement.

416
00:26:02,767 --> 00:26:05,300
API-002 · Follow-up 1

417
00:26:06,425 --> 00:26:09,953
How would <ui-form-field> give the input its aria-describedby

418
00:26:09,953 --> 00:26:13,040
without the consumer writing ids by hand?

419
00:26:18,461 --> 00:26:23,301
Let the form field and projected control communicate through a small injected contract.

420
00:26:23,301 --> 00:26:27,769
The field owns unique identifiers for helper and error text; the control

421
00:26:27,769 --> 00:26:31,865
directive merges them with any consumer-provided descriptions on the native element.

422
00:26:31,865 --> 00:26:36,706
Register and unregister as content changes, and keep invalid and label state synchronized.

423
00:26:36,706 --> 00:26:39,684
Do not overwrite the consumer's existing aria-describedby values.

424
00:26:39,684 --> 00:26:44,153
Test multiple fields and dynamic helper or error content through a host.

425
00:26:45,528 --> 00:26:47,898
API-002 · Follow-up 2

426
00:26:49,023 --> 00:26:52,540
When is wrapping the native element still the better choice?

427
00:26:57,940 --> 00:27:01,754
Wrapping is appropriate for a genuinely composite widget with coordinated elements,

428
00:27:01,754 --> 00:27:05,568
such as a date picker with a popup trigger or a

429
00:27:05,568 --> 00:27:08,689
control whose internal structure is intrinsic to its behavior.

430
00:27:08,689 --> 00:27:11,810
It can also enforce a carefully documented layout contract.

431
00:27:11,810 --> 00:27:15,625
The cost is forwarding native capabilities, focus, labels, and forms integration.

432
00:27:15,625 --> 00:27:19,093
If the wrapper mainly adds a border to one input,

433
00:27:19,093 --> 00:27:22,907
an attribute directive usually gives a smaller and more adaptable API.

434
00:27:22,907 --> 00:27:26,722
Choose according to the task and supported customization, not stylistic preference.

435
00:27:28,097 --> 00:27:30,910
API-002 · Follow-up 3

436
00:27:32,035 --> 00:27:36,610
Could hostDirectives help share behaviour between uiInput and other controls?

437
00:27:41,976 --> 00:27:47,764
Host directives can compose reusable behavior statically and expose selected inputs and outputs explicitly.

438
00:27:47,764 --> 00:27:53,552
They can help share focus, disabled state, or other host-level logic across native controls.

439
00:27:53,552 --> 00:27:57,686
They do not automatically forward arbitrary attributes to an internal

440
00:27:57,686 --> 00:28:01,407
input and do not replace the form-field relationship contract.

441
00:28:01,407 --> 00:28:04,714
Check construction and input precedence when composing directives.

442
00:28:04,714 --> 00:28:10,503
Document the exposed API so consumers know which capabilities belong to the public control.

